    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R7"],
      "pins": ["R7", "U4.19", "U4.20", "U2.35"],
      "nets": ["DISP_SDA", "VCC"],
      "observed": "On the DISPLAY sheet (display.kicad_sch, page 5), the pull-up resistor R7 on the DISP_SDA net (joining U4 D1/D2 pins 19/20 and the MCU PB14 label) is drawn with the value '10M' (10 MΩ).",
      "expected": "The specification states the DISP_SDA line uses a 10 kΩ pull-up: 'DISP_SDA: U2 pin 35 (PB14) — R7 (10 kΩ) to VCC — U4 pins 19/20 (D1/D2)'. A standard I2C pull-up is in the 1.5–10 kΩ range.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "display.kicad_sch (page 5): 'R7 10M' on the DISP_SDA pull-up, next to 'R6 10k' on DISP_SCL"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "DISP_SDA: U2 pin 35 (PB14) — R7 (10 kΩ) to VCC — U4 pins 19/20 (D1/D2)"}
      ],
      "impact": "A 10 MΩ pull-up is three orders of magnitude too weak to charge the SDA bus capacitance within the I2C timing budget and provides poor noise immunity; the SSD1306 I2C bus will not pull SDA high reliably, so the OLED display communication fails or is intermittent.",
      "confidence": 0.8
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic matches the specification across power rails, NFC/display/touch connectivity, SWD header, and most passives, but R7, the DISP_SDA pull-up, is drawn as 10 MΩ instead of the specified 10 kΩ, which would break I2C communication with the OLED display."
}

